GitHub Issue:
Post Date: 2016-01-07T16:02:33Z
Current Date: April 4 2025
Title: Computation Scheduler stopping/slowing items from processing
Description: I am having a problem with trying to multi-thread my application to improve performance. My actual application follows a similar structure to the code below (except using real data and doing real work). I am using a flatMap so that I can simultaneously process the items on mutliple threads since the map work can potentially take a lot of time. 

What I am seeing, is when an item takes a long time to process in the map, it stops the items from being processed by other threads in the pool. In this example, I am seeing 11 items waiting till after the "Done Sleeping..." to be processed. The point of the multi-threading is for those items to be all processed in parallel with the item that is processing slowly.

I noticed by switching to a FixedThreadPool Scheduler, it works as expected and the other threads pick up all the items remaining to be processed. 

Why does the computation scheduler act like this? Is it a bug, or is there something I am not understanding?

The code snippet is in Groovy and I am using RxJava 1.1.0.

```
import rx.Observable
import rx.Scheduler
import rx.schedulers.Schedulers

import java.util.concurrent.CountDownLatch
import java.util.concurrent.Executors

def threadpool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(8)
def latch = new CountDownLatch(1)

// Using a fixed thread pool works
//Scheduler scheduler = Schedulers.from(threadpool)

// Using the computation thread pool doesn't
Scheduler scheduler = Schedulers.computation()

Observable.from(1..100).flatMap {
    Observable.just(it).subscribeOn(scheduler).map {
        if (it == 5) {
            println "${Thread.currentThread().name} - Sleeping... ${it}"
            // simulate long work here - only on certain items
            Thread.sleep(15_000)
            println "${Thread.currentThread().name} - Done Sleeping... ${it}"
        }
        return it
    }
}.subscribe ({
    println "${Thread.currentThread().name} - Got a num: ${it}"
}, {
    println "Error"
    it.printStackTrace()
    threadpool.shutdown()
    latch.countDown()
}, {
    println 'Complete'
    threadpool.shutdown()
    latch.countDown()
})
latch.await()
```


